# 2.3 Information, Knowledge, and Agency

Human beings navigate their environments by constructing internal representations of external reality. These cognitive models allow individuals to anticipate consequences, evaluate alternatives, identify threats, and coordinate behavior with others. Decision-making therefore depends not upon perfect knowledge but upon sufficiently accurate approximations of reality.

Research within cognitive psychology suggests that humans continuously update these mental models through processes of prediction and error correction. New information modifies existing beliefs, allowing individuals to adapt behavior in response to changing environmental conditions. Although these models are inevitably incomplete, they remain indispensable for effective action.

Autonomy consequently depends upon epistemic accuracy.

When individuals possess materially accurate information regarding available choices, foreseeable risks, and probable outcomes, they retain meaningful control over their decisions. Conversely, systematic distortion of relevant information interferes with this adaptive process, increasing the probability that decisions will diverge from the individual's authentic preferences or interests.

This relationship between information and agency extends beyond individual cognition. Democratic participation, contractual agreements, medical treatment, financial transactions, and religious commitment all presuppose that individuals possess sufficient information to evaluate available alternatives. Without adequate informational foundations, choice becomes increasingly symbolic rather than substantive.

Accordingly, information should not be viewed merely as a resource but as an enabling condition for autonomy itself.

